Katana License — DRAFT, awaiting Ghost's approval (status line at the end)

Copyright (c) 2026 Paul Jensen ("Ghost"). All rights reserved.

Katana is a compiler from COBOL to Java with a proof of equivalence (this repository: the front end,
the Semantic IR, the lowering, the Intent IR, the passes, the ledger, the findings, the command line,
and the equivalence harness under oracle/ and bench/). The runtime under rt/ has its own terms
(rt/LICENSE): a migrated program belongs to the party it was migrated for, and the runtime it links
against is theirs to keep. These terms are for everything else.

1. No license by default. Except as granted in sections 2 and 3, no license is granted to use, copy,
   modify, distribute, run, or build upon any part of this work, for any purpose, by any person,
   company, institution, or government.

2. Teaching grant. An accredited educational institution may use this work, unmodified, to teach
   computer science to its enrolled students: in a course, a lecture, an assignment, or a student
   project done for credit. The grant covers reading the source, running the tool on programs the
   institution has the right to use, and discussing what it does. It does not cover sponsored or
   contract research, work for an outside party, a product or service, a spin-out or a startup, a
   government or national laboratory, or publishing the source. A student who uses this work under
   this grant may keep what they learned; they may not keep the work. The institution keeps this
   notice with every copy and names the copyright holder wherever the work is cited.

3. Evaluation grant. Any party may run this work for ninety days, on its own machines, on programs it
   has the right to use, to decide whether to license it. The results stay inside the evaluating
   party and are not published or used in production. No other right is granted by evaluating.

4. Everything else is by agreement. Use for a commercial purpose, by a government or a public body at
   any level, by a nonprofit in the course of paid work, or by anyone outside sections 2 and 3,
   requires a written agreement with the copyright holder, which carries an annual fee. "Commercial
   purpose" includes using the work to migrate programs for pay, offering it or its output as a
   service, and using it inside a product. Ask: the copyright holder's contact is in the repository's
   README.

5. What the customer keeps. Under an engagement, the migrated Java, its proof report and its ledger
   belong to the party the migration was done for, and the runtime inside their suite is theirs
   under rt/LICENSE, perpetually and without further fee. The annual fee is for using Katana, not
   for running what Katana produced.

6. Patents. No patent license is granted by these terms. Rights under any patent the copyright holder
   holds or obtains are granted only in a written agreement under section 4.

7. Third-party material. The ProLeap grammars under front/grammar/ are MIT and keep their notice;
   material under corpus/ and */prototypes/ carries its own license, listed in corpus/MANIFEST.md
   and each prototypes/README.md. Nothing under the GNU General Public License is in this work.

8. No warranty. This work is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind, and the copyright
   holder is not liable for damages arising from its use, except as a written agreement under
   section 4 states otherwise.

9. Termination. A grant under section 2 or 3 ends automatically on any use outside it.

